# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Follow the short parable of Mark 4:26–29 in the order Jesus tells it: seed cast into the ground, a man who sleeps and rises, growth in stages, and a sickle put in.',
    'Mark every part of the story that Jesus leaves unnamed, and see that He gives no explanation of it.',
    'State what the comparison says about the Kingdom of God, in the words of the story, without assigning an identity to the man, the seed, the ground, the earth or the harvest.',
    'Keep this parable apart from the Sower and from the Mustard Seed that stand beside it in Mark 4, and refuse to carry the meaning of one into another.',
    'Place the parable in Jesus’ earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and hold the boundary where the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins.',
    'State a principled lesson for today and say plainly why casting, sleeping and reaping in the story are not steps of salvation.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read Mark 4:26–29 exactly as Jesus gave it, to mark what He does not identify, and to state what the comparison says about the Kingdom of God without adding a referent, a symbol or a doctrine the text does not supply.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Mark 4 opens by the sea. “And he began again to teach by the sea side: and there was gathered unto him a great multitude, so that he entered into a ship, and sat in the sea; and the whole multitude was by the sea on the land” (4:1). “And he taught them many things by parables, and said unto them in his doctrine” (4:2). The Sower is the first parable of the day (4:3–9).'))
A(('p', '2.2 Where this parable stands. After the Sower comes a scene apart from the crowd: “And when he was alone, they that were about him with the twelve asked of him the parable” (4:10). Jesus answers them there (4:11–20). He then speaks of a candle and of the measure (4:21–25). Then, without a new scene being named, the text says, “And he said, So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground” (4:26). The growing seed follows the sayings of 4:21–25 and comes before the Mustard Seed (4:30–32).'))
A(('call', 'No Question Introduces This Parable',
   'No question or remark from a hearer comes before the growing seed. It begins with “And he said, So is the kingdom of God” (4:26). Mark does not say who is being addressed in these verses beyond what he says of 4:10 and of the summary at 4:33–34, and this study adds nothing to that.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The setting before the scene. Mark has already shown the opposition that stands behind the day of the parables. “And the Pharisees went forth, and straightway took counsel with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him” (3:6), and the scribes from Jerusalem said, “He hath Beelzebub, and by the prince of the devils casteth he out devils” (3:22). The growing seed is told after that refusal had been spoken.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Mark has said what Jesus preached: “Jesus came into Galilee, preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God, And saying, The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand: repent ye, and believe the gospel” (1:14–15). He sent out the Twelve (6:7), and “they went out, and preached that men should repent” (6:12). The parable uses the same words, “the kingdom of God,” and it speaks to people who have heard that the Kingdom is at hand.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Mark gives Jesus’ own statement about the parables of this day: “Unto you it is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of God: but unto them that are without, all these things are done in parables” (4:11). Mark closes the section by saying, “And with many such parables spake he the word unto them, as they were able to hear it. But without a parable spake he not unto them: and when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples” (4:33–34). The growing seed is part of that body of teaching. It is not a free-floating saying about growth in general.'))
A(('ctbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Verse', 'KJV text', 'What to observe'],
    ['Mark 4:1–2', '“a great multitude”; “he taught them many things by parables”', 'One day of many parables.'],
    ['Mark 4:10–11', '“Unto you it is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of God”', 'The scene apart, after the Sower.'],
    ['Mark 4:24–25', '“Take heed what ye hear”', 'The sayings that come just before.'],
    ['Mark 4:26', '“So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground”', 'The comparison begins.'],
    ['Mark 4:33–34', '“when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples”', 'What Mark says of Jesus’ teaching that day.'],
    ['Mark 1:14–15', '“the kingdom of God is at hand”', 'What was preached, and where.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The opening comparison (4:26). “And he said, So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground.” The comparison is to the kingdom of God “as if a man should cast seed into the ground.” The first act in the story is a man casting seed into the ground.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The man’s rest and the seed’s growth (4:27). “And should sleep, and rise night and day, and the seed should spring and grow up, he knoweth not how.” The man sleeps and rises, night and day. The seed springs and grows up. The text says of the man, “he knoweth not how.” It does not say that he is idle or careless, and it does not say that he was told how the seed grows.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The earth and the stages (4:28). “For the earth bringeth forth fruit of herself; first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear.” The text gives the cause as “the earth,” and it says the earth does so “of herself.” The growth then has three named stages: first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The harvest (4:29). “But when the fruit is brought forth, immediately he putteth in the sickle, because the harvest is come.” The sequence now turns on “But.” When the fruit is brought forth, the man immediately puts in the sickle, and the text gives the reason: “because the harvest is come.” The story ends with the sickle.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Mark 4:26', '“a man should cast seed into the ground”', 'The man acts once, at the start.'],
    ['Mark 4:27', '“sleep, and rise night and day”; “he knoweth not how”', 'Time passes; the growth is not the man’s knowledge.'],
    ['Mark 4:28', '“the earth bringeth forth fruit of herself”', 'The earth is named as the one that brings forth.'],
    ['Mark 4:28', '“first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear”', 'Three stages, in order.'],
    ['Mark 4:29', '“immediately he putteth in the sickle, because the harvest is come”', 'The man acts again when the fruit is ready.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.5 What the story contains. Seven things can be counted in the text: a man, seed, ground (earth), sleeping and rising, growth in three stages, a sickle and a harvest. The man acts at the beginning and at the end. Between the two acts the text speaks of time and of growth, and the growth is said to come from the earth and from the seed itself.'))
A(('p', '3.6 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the man is. Jesus does not say what the seed is. Jesus does not say what the ground or the earth is. Jesus does not say how long the sleeping and rising last. Jesus does not say what the fruit or the harvest is, or whose harvest it is. The story gives a sowing, a wait with growth, and a reaping, and it stops at the sickle.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. In Mark 4 Jesus explains the Sower (4:13–20). For the growing seed He gives none. Mark records no request for an explanation of it and no answer. Mark does say that Jesus “expounded all things to his disciples” when they were alone (4:34), but he does not set down what was said about this parable. This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not identify. The parable names some parts of the story and leaves each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the man. Jesus does not identify the seed. Jesus does not identify the ground or the earth. Jesus does not identify the sleeping and rising. Jesus does not identify the blade, the ear or the full corn. Jesus does not identify the sickle. Jesus does not identify the harvest.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the story', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['The man', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Mark 4:26–29'],
    ['The seed', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Mark 4:26–28'],
    ['The ground and the earth', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Mark 4:26, 28'],
    ['The sleeping and rising', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Mark 4:27'],
    ['The blade, the ear, the full corn', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Mark 4:28'],
    ['The sickle and the harvest', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Mark 4:29'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the man is a certain person, that the seed is a certain thing, that the ground is a certain people or that the harvest is a certain event is supplying what the Lord did not. “The sower soweth the word” (4:14) is Jesus’ statement about the Sower, and it is not carried into 4:26.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What the text does say. The text says that the Kingdom of God is so, as if a man should cast seed into the ground; that he sleeps and rises; that the seed springs and grows, he knoweth not how; that the earth brings forth fruit of herself, first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear; and that when the fruit is brought forth, he immediately puts in the sickle, because the harvest is come. These statements are made by the story itself and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for this parable, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus says the kingdom of God is so, as if a man should cast seed into the ground and, after sleeping and rising, put in the sickle when the harvest has come. The comparison shows a beginning made by a man, a growth that goes on without his knowing how, and a reaping when the fruit is ready. The meaning is in what the story shows of the beginning, the growth and the harvest.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the Kingdom.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The Kingdom has a beginning that is a casting of seed.** The man’s first act is to cast seed into the ground. The story begins with a sowing.',
    '**The growth proceeds apart from the man’s knowledge.** The seed springs and grows up, “he knoweth not how” (4:27). The time of waiting goes on while he sleeps and rises.',
    '**The growth comes from the earth and the seed.** “The earth bringeth forth fruit of herself” (4:28). The text does not say the man makes it grow.',
    '**The growth is ordered.** First the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear. The stages come one after another and none is skipped.',
    '**The harvest comes when the fruit is ready.** The man puts in the sickle “immediately” when the fruit is brought forth, “because the harvest is come” (4:29). The reaping follows the ripening and does not come before it.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers had heard that the Kingdom of God is at hand (1:15). They were meant to understand that the Kingdom they were told of has a sowing, a growth that goes on without a man’s understanding of it, and a harvest that comes in its time. The parable speaks of the course of the Kingdom, from a casting of seed to a harvest.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the contrast between the man’s two acts and the long stretch between them. He acts at the start and acts again at the end. The parable asks the hearer to see that what lies between is not worked out by the man’s watching or by his knowing.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The Kingdom begins in a sowing. Its growth goes on without the sower’s knowledge. Its growth is ordered and comes from the earth. Its harvest comes when the fruit is brought forth. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the man, the seed, the earth or the harvest represents anything.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say who the man is or what the seed is. It does not say that the harvest is any particular event. It does not turn casting, sleeping or reaping into steps by which a person is saved. It does not draw a meaning from another parable into this one.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The growing seed is one of the parables Mark records on the day he describes in chapter 4. It is kept apart from its neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Mark 4:3–9, 13–20', '“there went out a sower to sow”; “The sower soweth the word”', 'The Sower. Jesus explains it in 4:13–20. That explanation is for that parable only and is not carried into 4:26–29.'],
    ['Mark 4:30–32', '“a grain of mustard seed”', 'The Mustard Seed. A seed again, but a small seed that becomes greater than all herbs. No sleeping and rising, no stages of blade, ear and corn, and no sickle.'],
    ['Matt 13:24–30, 36–43', '“sowed good seed in his field”; “The field is the world”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. A field appears, an enemy appears, and Jesus explains it. Neither the enemy nor the explanation is part of the growing seed.'],
    ['Joel 3:13; Rev 14:15', '“Put ye in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe”; “Thrust in thy sickle, and reap”', 'Other places where a sickle and a harvest are named, each in its own setting. They are not an explanation of 4:29.'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The growing seed stands in Mark 4 between the sayings of 4:21–25 and the Mustard Seed. The Wheat and the Weeds is told in Matthew 13 and explained in the house (13:36). A shared word such as “seed” or “harvest” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The growing seed has a man and the earth. The Sower has a sower and four kinds of ground. The Wheat and the Weeds has a sower, an enemy, servants and reapers. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The growing seed moves from casting, through sleeping and growth, to the sickle. The Sower moves from sowing to the fate of the seed on different ground. The Mustard Seed moves from a small seed to a large plant. None of those movements is read into this one.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “seed” in 4:26 is not “the word” of 4:14, which Jesus says of the Sower. Second, “the ground” in 4:26 is not one of the kinds of ground in the Sower. Third, the sickle and harvest of 4:29 are not the harvest of Matthew 13:30 or 13:39, and the passages of Joel 3:13 and Revelation 14:15 are not an explanation of this verse.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of God is at hand (1:14–15) and sent the Twelve to preach repentance (6:7, 12). The growing seed belongs to that ministry. The Kingdom it speaks of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel, and Mark places the parable among the teaching of that day (4:33–34).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The “mystery of the kingdom of God” that Jesus says is given to the disciples (4:11) is His own Kingdom matter given to them during His earthly ministry. It is not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul. The word is shared and the subject is not.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The man, the seed, the earth and the harvest are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The man’s casting of seed and his putting in of the sickle are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of patience.** The man sleeps and rises while the seed grows. A reader learns that some growth is not hurried or controlled by watching it.',
    '**A lesson of order.** The stages come one after another: the blade, the ear, the full corn. A reader learns not to look for the full corn in the blade.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** The man “knoweth not how.” A reader learns that he may do his part without understanding all that goes on beyond it.',
    '**A lesson of readiness.** When the fruit is brought forth the man acts at once. A reader learns that what is ready calls for a response and not for delay.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s course is not turned into the Body’s hope, and the harvest is not turned into a requirement laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus gives one short comparison. The kingdom of God is so, as if a man should cast seed into the ground, sleep and rise night and day, and the seed should spring and grow up, he knoweth not how. The earth brings forth fruit of herself, first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear. When the fruit is brought forth, the man immediately puts in the sickle, because the harvest is come. Jesus gives no explanation of the story and names none of its parts. The comparison says that the Kingdom begins in a sowing, that its growth goes on without the sower’s knowledge and in order, and that its harvest comes when the fruit is ready. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the man is, what the seed is, what the earth is or what the harvest is, or that makes the casting and the reaping a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a man casts seed into the ground; he sleeps and rises while the seed grows by stages, he knoweth not how; when the fruit is ready he puts in the sickle.',
    '**The meaning:** the Kingdom begins in a sowing; its growth goes on without the sower’s knowledge and in order; its harvest comes when the fruit is ready.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the man, the seed, the earth or the harvest; the explanation of the Sower is not the explanation of this parable.',
    '**The present lesson:** patience, order, humility and readiness, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

